Let an interrupted thunk be forced again on Determinate Nix.

Upstream 5c4f498d3 (in 2.35) restores a thunk that an `Interrupted`
stopped, so a later force evaluates it again (nixidae/nixidae#634).
Determinate Nix forces a thunk through its own concurrent state
machine, which stores every exception as `Value::Failed`, an interrupt
included. So a cancelled huggorm call poisoned the value for good:
test_an_interrupted_value_evaluates_again raised `Interrupted` again.

The owner puts the thunk back and wakes the threads that wait on it.
`waitOnThunk` returns what it finds, and a waiter that finds no final
value raises `Interrupted`: the cancel stops the work under it, as on
one core (test_an_interrupted_value_another_thread_awaits_evaluates_again,
nixidae/nixidae#1082). A waiter that forced the thunk again instead
kept the cancelled work running, and an evaluator thread then called a
Python primop while Python finalized: `Fatal Python error:
gilstate_tss_set`.

A waiter that is interrupted owns nothing and leaves the value alone.
Unpatched, it stored `Failed` in a thunk another thread still ran.

Applies after nix-determinate-failed-value-releases-its-error.
